  "coverage_qualification": [
    "All statements concern the actual sealed original current and test spaces. The literal final topology is unchanged.",
    "The flat form seminorm, extended dual supremum, complete finite-flat carriers, flat closure spaces and current series have independent specifications and proved existence before their opaque seals.",
    "The original finite-flat current statement includes support and continuity. Its completeness proof does not assume the underlying test seminorm is definite.",
    "The sharp mass minimum is attained, including infinite values. Finite input mass gives the original normal minimising filling.",
    "The full compact-index union, boundary closure, scalar multiplication, pointwise local criterion and prescribed-neighbourhood localisation retain the original hypotheses and support indices.",
    "All scalar estimates retain the original partial-product domains; no value is assigned to an undefined zero-times-infinity product.",
    "Both Cartesian estimates retain the exact binomial(i+j+2,i+1) coefficient. Normal-factor flat closure and both degree-zero endpoints are proved.",
    "The actual dyadic interval series has geometric mass tails and flat boundary. Genuine bounded endpoint tests prove infinite boundary mass. The exact support carries no nonzero normal one-current, so its flat and mass closures agree, excluding that boundary from flatness at its own support index.",
    "The actual weighted interval series retains the printed endpoints, weights and support interval. A common scalar coordinate calibration proves its exact finite mass 2 sum(k^-2).",
    "The actual independent smooth cutoff family has the original strict supports, unit comass, derivative bounds and endpoint values. The literal finite product tests have comass at most one and derivative comass at most two.",
    "The product-current actions are the original harmonic partial sums. Their unboundedness proves that both original products are not flat, using the previously proved boundary identity.",
    "The actual nonzero R^0 point source has mass one and singleton flat value one. Its empty-index flat value is infinite.",
    "No new mathematical source error was found. No unpublished source was used or cited. The forward nonflat finite-mass example remains required under 4.1.15.",
    "Items 4.1.13\u20134.1.34 remain required and pending."
